Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>16  Reminders, printing and sharing</H1>
<HR>
The organiser from chapter 15 already keeps a diary, things to do, addresses, notes, a year planner and
anniversaries. This chapter adds what turns it into a finished application: alarms that remind you of
appointments, a Find that looks in every section, printing, swapping entries with other programs, and
choices you can change. At the end is the finished <CODE>!Organiser</CODE>, a tour of it, and some ideas for
taking it further.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Organiser" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Reminders</H2>
An appointment can have an alarm: <I>At the time</I>, or 5 minutes, 15 minutes, an hour or a day before,
chosen in the appointment's dialogue box (chapter 12) and kept as a number of minutes. Until now nothing looked at it. RISC OS has its own alarm program, !Alarm, and the
Organiser's alarms work the same way: at the right time a box appears with the appointment in it, and two
buttons - <I>OK</I>, and <I>Snooze</I> to be reminded again in five minutes.<P>

<H3>The plan</H3>
A desktop program can't sit in a loop waiting for the time to come round (book 1, chapter 9). Instead it asks
to be called now and then with <CODE>task.every</CODE>, and each time looks at the clock:<P>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Every ten seconds, go through the appointments today and tomorrow in every open organiser.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Work out when each one's alarm goes off: its time, less the alarm's minutes.<P>
<DT><DD>3  If that time has come, and the alarm hasn't been shown yet, show it.<P>
</DL>
Two things need remembering: which alarms have been shown (so each appears once), and which have been
snoozed (so they come back later). An alarm is named by its document, its appointment and its date together - a
repeating appointment has an alarm on every day it happens. The document is given a number of its own for
this rather than using its filename, which changes when an untitled document is saved.<P>
One more rule, from !Alarm: an alarm more than an hour old has been <I>missed</I> (the computer was off, say)
and isn't shown. Without it, opening an organiser in the evening would bring up every alarm of the day at
once.<P>

<H3>The Reminders module</H3>
<PRE>
// Reminders - the alarms: every few seconds, look for appointments
// whose alarm time has come, and show a box for each, like !Alarm's:
// OK, or Snooze to be reminded again in five minutes. An alarm more
// than an hour old (the computer was off) is missed, not shown.
//
//   const r = new Reminders(app); r.check(now)   (now: a Date)
import { beep } from 'riscos';
import { Form } from './Toolkit/Form';
import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates';

const SNOOZE = 5;                    // minutes
const LATE = 60;                     // older alarms have been missed

export class Reminders {
  constructor(app) {
    this.app = app;
    this.done = new Set();           // alarms already shown
    this.snoozed = new Map();        // alarm -&gt; when to show again
    this.ids = new WeakMap();        // a number for each document
    this.count = 0;
    app.task.every(10000, () =&gt; this.check(new Date()));
  }
  /** A document's number: unlike its name, it stays the same when an
   * untitled document is saved, and two untitled ones differ. */
  idOf(doc) {
    if (!this.ids.has(doc)) this.ids.set(doc, ++this.count);
    return this.ids.get(doc);
  }

  /** Show the alarms that are due at the time now. */
  check(now) {
    const today = D.keyOf(now), tomorrow = D.addDays(today, 1);
    for (const doc of this.app.docs) {
      for (const { appt, date } of doc.diary.occurrences(today,
        tomorrow)) {
        if (appt.alarm == null || !appt.time) continue;
        const name = `${this.idOf(doc)}:${appt.id}:${date}`;
        const at = this.snoozed.get(name) ?? alarmTime(appt, date);
        const due = now &gt;= at &amp;&amp; now - at &lt; LATE * 60000;
        if (due &amp;&amp; !this.done.has(name)) {
          this.done.add(name);
          this.show(name, appt, date);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  async show(name, appt, date) {
    beep();
    const box = new Form(this.app.task, {
      title: 'Organiser alarm', fields: [],
      message: `${D.format(date, '%W3 %ZDY %M3')}, ${appt.time}: ` +
        appt.text,
      buttons: ['Snooze', 'OK'],
    });
    const r = await box.run();
    box.win.delete();
    if (r?.button === 'Snooze') {    // again in five minutes
      this.snoozed.set(name, new Date(Date.now() + SNOOZE * 60000));
      this.done.delete(name);
    }
  }
}

/** When an appointment's alarm goes off on a day. */
export function alarmTime(appt, date) {
  const [h, m] = appt.time.split(':').map(Number);
  const d = D.dateOf(date);
  d.setHours(h, m - appt.alarm, 0, 0);
  return d;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>done</CODE> is a <CODE>Set</CODE> of the names of alarms already shown, and <CODE>snoozed</CODE> a
<CODE>Map</CODE> from an alarm's name to the time it should come back. <CODE>check</CODE> asks each open
document's diary for the <CODE>occurrences</CODE> of appointments from today to tomorrow - the same method
the Diary pages use to draw repeating appointments (chapter 12) - so repeats get their alarms too.<P>
<CODE>idOf</CODE> hands out those numbers, keeping them in a <CODE>WeakMap</CODE> (a <CODE>Map</CODE> that
lets go of a document once it's closed). <CODE>alarmTime</CODE> builds a <CODE>Date</CODE> for the day and
sets its hours and minutes with <CODE>setHours(h, m, s, ms)</CODE>, which takes the hours and, optionally,
the minutes, seconds and milliseconds. For an
appointment at 09:10 with a 15-minute alarm, the minutes are <CODE>10 - 15</CODE>, which is -5.
<CODE>setHours</CODE> doesn't mind: it carries the minutes over, and the time becomes 08:55. Letting
<CODE>Date</CODE> do the carrying is much simpler (and safer) than doing it yourself.<P>
<CODE>show</CODE> uses a <CODE>Form</CODE> with no fields at all - just a message and two buttons - as a
small alarm box, and sounds the bell with <CODE>beep</CODE>. <CODE>run</CODE> waits for a button; if it was
<I>Snooze</I>, the alarm gets a new time five minutes on and is taken out of <CODE>done</CODE>, so a later
<CODE>check</CODE> shows it again.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/OrganiserAlarm" WIDTH=434 HEIGHT=145></CENTER>
<CODE>!RunImage</CODE> makes one <CODE>Reminders</CODE> for the whole application, and checks straight away
whenever a file is opened, rather than waiting up to ten seconds:<P>
<PRE>
  const app = {
    task, TYPE,
    docs: new Set(),                 // the open organiser files
    prefs: await loadChoices(),
    area: sprites.spritesFromFile(await vfs.readFile(ctx.dir +
      '.Sprites')),                  // the toolbox's pictures
    async open(path) {
      const name = vfs.canonical(path);
      const open = [...app.docs].find((d) =&gt; d.filename === name);
      if (open) {
        open.view.win.open({ behind: 'top' });
        return open;
      }
      try {
        const doc = await Document.load(app, name);
        app.reminders.check(new Date());       // any alarms due now?
        return doc;
      } catch (e) {
        reportError(`Can't load ${vfs.leaf(name)}: ${e.message}`);
        return null;
      }
    },
    redrawAll() { for (const d of app.docs) d.view.redraw(); },
    get modified() { return [...app.docs].some((d) =&gt; d.modified); },
  };
  app.reminders = new Reminders(app);
</PRE>
<B>Note</B>: an alarm only goes off while the Organiser is running, with the file open - just as !Alarm's
alarms only go off while !Alarm is loaded.<P>

