<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Finding</H2>
Lotus Organizer's Find looked through every section at once, and the Organiser's does too (F4, the magnifying
glass in the toolbox, or <I>Menu &gt; Find</I>). Each section knows best how to search its own entries, so the
job is shared out: the <CODE>Section</CODE> class every page is built on (chapter 10) gets one more method,
which finds nothing -<P>
<PRE>
  today() {}
  newEntry() {}
  find() { return []; }
  go() {}
</PRE>
- and each page replaces it with its own. The Diary's looks in the appointments' text:<P>
<PRE>
  find(s) {
    const t = s.toLowerCase();
    return this.doc.diary.all()
      .filter((a) =&gt; a.text.toLowerCase().includes(t))
      .map((a) =&gt; ({ text: this.describe({ id: a.id }),
        ref: { section: 'diary', id: a.id, date: a.date } }));
  }
</PRE>
Each result has the text to show in a list, and a <CODE>ref</CODE> saying where the entry is: its section,
its id, and (for the diary) its date. That's the same kind of reference links use (chapter 14), so
<CODE>org.go(ref)</CODE> can already turn to it. The Address page doesn't even need to search: it asks the
address book, whose <CODE>list({ search })</CODE> from chapter 6 does exactly this.<P>
Find itself, in <CODE>Tools</CODE>, asks what to look for, collects every section's results and shows them
in a list:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ finding
let findForm = null;
export async function find(org) {
  findForm ??= new Form(org.app.task, { title: 'Find',
    fields: [{ name: 'text', label: 'Find' }],
    buttons: ['Cancel', 'Find'] });
  const v = await findForm.ask({});
  if (!v?.text) return;
  const found = org.sections.flatMap((s) =&gt; s.find(v.text)
    .map((f) =&gt; ({ ...f, where: s.name })));
  if (!found.length) {
    reportError(`'${v.text}' wasn't found`);
    return;
  }
  const w = org.app.task.createWindow({ title: `Found '${v.text}'`,
    w: 420, h: 240, extent: { w: 420, h: 240 },
    colours: { workBg: 0 },
    workButton: 'clickdragdouble',
    flags: { back: true, close: true, title: true, vscroll: true,
      size: true, moveable: true },
    help: 'Double-click an entry to go to it.' });
  const list = new ListView(w, { key: (f) =&gt; found.indexOf(f),
    columns: [{ title: 'Section', width: 100, text: (f) =&gt; f.where },
      { title: 'Entry', width: 300, text: (f) =&gt; f.text }] });
  list.setItems(found);
  list.on('open', (f) =&gt; org.go(f.ref));
  w.open({ behind: 'top' });
}
</PRE>
<CODE>flatMap</CODE> (chapter 9) joins the sections' lists into one, and <CODE>{ ...f, where: s.name
}</CODE> adds the section's name to each result for the first column. The results window is the
<CODE>ListView</CODE> from chapter 2 again, in a window of its own: double-click a result and the organiser
turns to it.<P>
