<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Printing</H2>
The toolbox's printer button (and the Print key, and <I>Menu &gt; File &gt; Print pages</I>) prints the two
pages showing. The pages are drawn by one method, <CODE>org.draw(g)</CODE>, whatever <CODE>g</CODE> is - so
printing them only needs a canvas of their own to draw on:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ printing
export function print(org) {
  if (!os.printers) {
    reportError('To print, load !Printers first (it is in Apps)');
    return;
  }
  // draw the pages again, on a canvas of their own
  const b = org.binder;
  const c = document.createElement('canvas');
  c.width = b.w; c.height = b.h;
  const g = c.getContext('2d');
  g.translate(-b.x, -b.y);
  org.draw(g);
  // a notepad page's text is on a canvas of its own: copy it in too
  const a = org.section.area;
  if (a) g.drawImage(a.canvas, a.x, a.y, a.w, a.h);
  os.printers.print({ title: `Organiser: ${org.section.name}`,
    canvas: c });
}
</PRE>
<CODE>document</CODE> is the desktop's object for making things like canvases, and
<CODE>document.createElement('canvas')</CODE> makes a canvas that isn't in any window: a sheet of paper the
program can draw on without anything appearing on the screen. It's made the size of the binder, and
<CODE>translate</CODE> moves the drawing so the binder's corner is at its top left. Then the canvas goes to
!Printers, which prints it on the printer chosen there (see appendix C). A Notepad page's text is on the
text area's own canvas (chapter 14), not drawn by <CODE>org.draw</CODE>, so <CODE>drawImage</CODE> copies it
onto the sheet in its place.<P>
Printing needs !Printers to be running, as it does for every RISC OS program. If it isn't,
<CODE>os.printers</CODE> is missing, and the Organiser says what to do rather than failing.<P>
<B>Note</B>: this is the first time drawing code has been used for two things - the screen and the printer.
It works because <CODE>draw</CODE> only draws: it doesn't change anything or depend on the window. Keeping
drawing like that pays off.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Swapping with other programs</H2>
Most calendar programs can read and write <I>iCalendar</I> files (<CODE>.ics</CODE>). The last toolkit
module, <CODE>ICal</CODE>, handles a small part of the format: appointments (<CODE>VEVENT</CODE>), things to
do (<CODE>VTODO</CODE>), repeats and alarms. Appendix E describes exactly what it writes and reads. This is
how an appointment goes out:<P>
<PRE>
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1790371712345-1@organiser
DTSTAMP:20260925T093000Z
DTSTART:20260926T123000
DTEND:20260926T133000
SUMMARY:Lunch with Bob
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Lunch with Bob
TRIGGER:-PT15M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
</PRE>
Each line is a name, a colon and a value; <CODE>BEGIN</CODE> and <CODE>END</CODE> bracket an entry. It's the
same idea as vCard (chapter 8), and <CODE>icalWrite</CODE> is written the same way, pushing lines onto an
array:<P>
<PRE>
export function icalWrite({ events = [], todos = [] }) {
  const out = ['BEGIN:VCALENDAR', 'VERSION:2.0',
    'PRODID:-//RISC OS//Organiser//EN'];
  let n = 0;
  const uid = () =&gt; `${Date.now()}-${++n}@organiser`;
  const now = stamp();
  for (const e of events) {
    out.push('BEGIN:VEVENT', `UID:${uid()}`, `DTSTAMP:${now}`);
    if (e.time) {
      out.push(`DTSTART:${at(e.date, e.time)}`);
      if (e.end) out.push(`DTEND:${at(e.date, e.end)}`);
    } else out.push(`DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:${day(e.date)}`);
    out.push(`SUMMARY:${esc(e.text ?? '')}`);
    if (e.repeat) {
      const { every, count, until } = e.repeat;
      let rule = `RRULE:FREQ=${FREQ[every]}`;
      // UNTIL is a date, or a (local) date and time if DTSTART is
      if (until) {
        rule += `;UNTIL=${e.time ? at(until, '23:59') : day(until)}`;
      } else if (count) rule += `;COUNT=${count}`;
      out.push(rule);
    }
    if (e.alarm != null) {
      out.push('BEGIN:VALARM', 'ACTION:DISPLAY',
        `DESCRIPTION:${esc(e.text ?? '')}`,
        `TRIGGER:-PT${e.alarm}M`, 'END:VALARM');
    }
    out.push('END:VEVENT');
  }
  for (const t of todos) {
    out.push('BEGIN:VTODO', `UID:${uid()}`, `DTSTAMP:${now}`,
      `SUMMARY:${esc(t.text ?? '')}`,
      `PRIORITY:${PRIORITY[t.priority] ?? 0}`,
      `STATUS:${t.done ? 'COMPLETED' : 'NEEDS-ACTION'}`);
    if (t.start) out.push(`DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:${day(t.start)}`);
    if (t.due) out.push(`DUE;VALUE=DATE:${day(t.due)}`);
    if (t.category) out.push(`CATEGORIES:${esc(t.category)}`);
    out.push('END:VTODO');
  }
  out.push('END:VCALENDAR');
  return out.map(fold).join('\r\n') + '\r\n';
}
</PRE>
Times are written as <CODE>20260926T123000</CODE> - the date, a <CODE>T</CODE>, and the time - by the small
functions <CODE>day</CODE> and <CODE>at</CODE> at the top of the module; <CODE>DTEND</CODE>, the end time,
goes out only if the appointment has one. <CODE>DTSTAMP</CODE>, which iCalendar asks for in every entry,
says when the file was made, in UTC (the <CODE>Z</CODE>): <CODE>toISOString</CODE> gives
<CODE>2026-09-25T09:30:00.000Z</CODE>, and <CODE>stamp</CODE> takes out the dashes, colons and
milliseconds. A repeat becomes an <CODE>RRULE</CODE> such as <CODE>FREQ=WEEKLY;COUNT=10</CODE> - or, if it
has an end date, <CODE>UNTIL</CODE> written the same way as <CODE>DTSTART</CODE>, but never both. An
alarm becomes a <CODE>VALARM</CODE> whose <CODE>TRIGGER</CODE> is the alarm's minutes before. iCalendar's priorities go from 1 to 9, so the Organiser's 1,
2 and 3 go out as 1, 5 and 9. Every line is folded at 75 characters, as vCard's are, by importing <CODE>fold</CODE> from chapter 8's
<CODE>Formats</CODE> rather than writing it again.<P>
<CODE>!Organiser</CODE>'s <CODE>Toolkit.list</CODE> gains two lines: <CODE>ListView</CODE>, for Find's
results, and <CODE>ICal</CODE>.<P>
Reading, in <CODE>icalRead</CODE>, uses destructuring in two new ways. Destructuring can <I>assign</I> as
well as declare: with no <CODE>const</CODE> or <CODE>let</CODE> in front, it puts the array's items into
variables or properties that already exist - here setting two properties at once:<P>
<PRE>
[item.date, item.time] = when(value);
</PRE>
and, as in chapter 12's answer 3, <I>rest</I> in destructuring collects everything not named into a new
object:<P>
<PRE>
const { kind, date, time, end, ...rest } = item;
</PRE>
Here <CODE>rest</CODE> is <CODE>item</CODE> without those four properties - just right for a to-do, which has
no date or time of its own. (Appendix A has both.)<P>

<H3>Import and export</H3>
<I>Menu &gt; File &gt; Export</I> saves the diary and to-dos as an iCalendar file, or the addresses as
vCards, each through a Save box as in chapter 8. Going the other way, files dragged onto the organiser's
window are read according to what's in them:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ other programs
export async function importFiles(org, files) {
  const doc = org.doc;
  for (const f of files) {
    if (f.filetype === org.app.TYPE) { await org.app.open(f.path);
      continue; }
    const text = decode(await vfs.readFile(f.path));
    if (/BEGIN:VCALENDAR/i.test(text)) {
      const { events, todos } = icalRead(text);
      for (const e of events) doc.diary.add(e);
      for (const t of todos) doc.todo.add(t);
    } else if (/BEGIN:VCARD/i.test(text)) {
      for (const c of vcardRead(text)) doc.book.add(c);
    } else {
      for (const c of csvRead(text)) doc.book.add(c);
    }
  }
}
export function exportMenu(org) {
  const doc = org.doc;
  const box = (name, type, data) =&gt; () =&gt; saveAs({
    task: org.app.task, filename: name, filetype: type,
    getData: async () =&gt; encode(data()) });
  return new Menu('Export', [
    { text: 'Diary and to do (iCalendar)', submenu: box('Diary/ics',
      0xFFF, () =&gt; icalWrite({ events: doc.diary.all(),
        todos: doc.todo.all() })) },
    { text: 'Addresses (vCard)', submenu: box('Cards/vcf', 0xFFF,
      () =&gt; vcardWrite(doc.book.all())) },
  ]);
}
</PRE>
<CODE>importFiles</CODE> opens an Organiser file as a document, and otherwise decodes the file (UTF-8 or
Latin-1, with <CODE>decode</CODE> from chapter 8) and looks inside: <CODE>BEGIN:VCALENDAR</CODE> means
iCalendar, <CODE>BEGIN:VCARD</CODE> vCards, and anything else is taken to be CSV from a spreadsheet. The
vCard and CSV code is chapter 8's <CODE>Formats</CODE>, unchanged; the new entries go straight into the
document's diary, to-do list or address book, whose <CODE>change</CODE> events redraw the pages and put
the <CODE>*</CODE> in the title. The window only needs to pass the files on:<P>
<PRE>
    w.on('dataload', (ev) =&gt; { tools.importFiles(this, ev.files);
      return true; });
</PRE>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Choices</H2>
The Organiser's choices follow chapter 9's pattern exactly: a <I>Choices...</I> item on the icon bar menu, a
dialogue box with <I>Default</I>, <I>Cancel</I>, <I>Set</I> and <I>Save</I>, and the settings kept with
<CODE>choices.read</CODE> and <CODE>choices.write</CODE> in <CODE>Choices:Organiser</CODE>. There are two:
the colour of the binder's cover, and whether to ask before deleting.<P>
<PRE>
// Choices - the settings a user can change, kept in Choices:Organiser
// (the RISC OS Style Guide's way): the binder's colour, and whether
// to ask before deleting.
//
//   const prefs = await loadChoices();     { colour, confirm }
//   openChoices(task, prefs, changed)    the Choices... dialogue box
import { choices } from 'riscos';
import { Form } from './Toolkit/Form';

export const COLOURS = { Brown: '#6b3f22', Black: '#2a2a2a',
  Blue: '#2c4a73', Green: '#2f5d3a', Red: '#7a2323' };
export const DEFAULTS = { colour: COLOURS.Brown, confirm: true };

export const loadChoices = () =&gt; choices.read('Organiser', DEFAULTS);

let form = null;
const nameOf = (c) =&gt; Object.keys(COLOURS)
  .find((n) =&gt; COLOURS[n] === c) ?? 'Brown';
const shown = (p) =&gt; ({ ...p, colour: nameOf(p.colour) });

export async function openChoices(task, prefs, changed) {
  form ??= new Form(task, {
    title: 'Organiser choices',
    help: 'Choose how the Organiser looks and behaves.',
    fields: [
      { name: 'colour', label: 'Binder', width: 120,
        choices: Object.keys(COLOURS),
        help: 'The colour of the binder\'s cover.' },
      { name: 'confirm', label: 'Ask before deleting', option: true },
    ],
    buttons: ['Default', 'Cancel', 'Set', 'Save'],
    stay: ['Default'],
  });
  form.off?.();
  form.off = form.on('button', () =&gt; form.set(shown(DEFAULTS)));
  const r = await form.run(shown(prefs));
  if (!r) return;
  Object.assign(prefs, { colour: COLOURS[r.values.colour] ??
    DEFAULTS.colour, confirm: r.values.confirm });
  if (r.button === 'Save') choices.write('Organiser', prefs);
  changed(prefs);
}
</PRE>
The box shows a colour's name (<I>Brown</I>) while the program keeps the colour itself
(<CODE>'#6b3f22'</CODE>); <CODE>nameOf</CODE> finds the name for a colour, and <CODE>shown</CODE> turns the
settings into what the box shows, as <CODE>shown</CODE> did in Contacts. When the choices are set,
<CODE>changed</CODE> redraws every open organiser, and the binder's drawing code picks up the colour:<P>
<PRE>
  draw(g) {
    this.binder.colour = this.app.prefs.colour;
    this.binder.draw(g, this.current);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/OrganiserChoices" WIDTH=409 HEIGHT=173></CENTER>
<B>Note</B>: the binder's colour is only used <I>inside</I> the window's work area, as the Style Guide asks:
the window's frame, the menus and the dialogue boxes all keep the desktop's own colours.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Help</H2>
Interactive help has been there since the first organiser, because every toolbox button, window and menu item
was given its <CODE>help</CODE> text as it was added (chapter 9 showed how). The new buttons are no
different:<P>
<PRE>
    { name: 'find', sprite: 'tb_find', action: () =&gt; find(org),
      help: 'Click SELECT to find entries in every section (F4).' },
    { name: 'print', sprite: 'tb_print', action: () =&gt; print(org),
      help: 'Click SELECT to print the pages showing.' },
</PRE>
The last thing is the <CODE>!Help</CODE> file, which says what the Organiser is and how to use it - short,
and in the order someone meets things:<P>
<PRE>
Organiser
=========

A personal organiser: a diary, things to do, an address book, a
notepad, a year planner and anniversaries, in a ring binder. It is the
program built in the book &quot;Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript&quot;
($.Manuals.JSApps), chapters 10 to 16.

Click SELECT on the icon bar icon for a new organiser, or double-click
an Organiser file. Click a coloured tab to open a section, and a
turned-up page corner to turn the page.

The toolbox, down the left:
  New        add an entry to the section that is open (or Insert)
  Link       then click two entries to link them; a chain appears on
             linked entries - click it to go to the other entry
  Back       go back to where you were
  Today      today's date: click it to go to today (or Home)
  views      the section's views (the Diary's Week, Day, Month, Year)
  Find       find entries in every section (F4)
  Print      print the pages showing (!Printers must be loaded)
  Bin        drag an entry here to delete it (or choose it, then
             press Delete)

In the Diary, double-click a day to add an appointment, double-click
an appointment to change it, and drag it to another day to move it.
Planner bars, anniversaries and things due to be done show on their
days. In the Planner, choose a colour in the key, then drag across the
days. In the Notepad, click a title on the contents page and type;
Ctrl-D types today's date.

Keys: F3 save, F4 find, Page Up and Page Down turn the pages,
Ctrl-1 to Ctrl-6 open the sections, Delete deletes the chosen
entry.

Menu &gt; File &gt; Export saves the diary and things to do as an iCalendar
file, and the addresses as vCards, for other programs; drag such files
onto the window to add what is in them.

Choices... on the icon bar menu sets the binder's colour, and
whether to ask before deleting.

Check tests the Organiser's parts that don't need windows.
</PRE>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>The finished organiser</H2>
That completes <CODE>!Organiser</CODE>. Here is what it does, and the chapter that added each part:<P>
<UL>
<LI>A ring binder: coloured section tabs, turned-up page corners, pages drawn as paper (chapter 10). Its
own file type, &amp;1C5, holding every section in one file, and several files open at once (chapter 10).<P>
<LI>The Address section: Contacts' own <CODE>Book</CODE> and card, reused unchanged (chapter 10).<P>
<LI>A diary with year, month, week and day views, moved about with the keys, and the toolbox down the left
(chapter 11).<P>
<LI>Appointments that repeat and can be dragged to another day, anniversaries, and the wastebasket
(chapter 12).<P>
<LI>Things to do, with priorities and due dates, shown in the diary (chapter 13).<P>
<LI>A notepad, links between entries, and Back (chapter 14).<P>
<LI>A year planner painted with coloured, patterned bars (chapter 15).<P>
<LI>Alarms, Find, printing, iCalendar and vCard, choices and help (this chapter).<P>
</UL>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/OrganiserMenu" WIDTH=1024 HEIGHT=768></CENTER>
The <CODE>Check</CODE> program has grown with it: it now tests the alarm times and that an appointment and a
to-do come back from iCalendar exactly as they went out. Run it after any change to the parts that don't need
windows.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Going further</H2>
The Organiser is yours to change. Some ideas, roughly from easiest to hardest:<P>
<DL>
<DT>A choice of first day of the week
<DD>The diary's weeks start on Monday (<CODE>startOfWeek</CODE> in <CODE>Dates</CODE>). Some people want
Sunday. That's a third choice, and a parameter for <CODE>startOfWeek</CODE>.<P>
<DT>A Calls section
<DD>Lotus Organizer added one in its second version: a log of telephone calls - who, when, what about, and
whether to call back. It's a new <CODE>Section</CODE> subclass, a collection in <CODE>Store</CODE>, and a
seventh tab. Everything else - saving, Find, links, the wastebasket - works through the section
interface.<P>
<DT>Sections of your own
<DD>Let the user add sections: a second to-do list for a project, say, with a name and a tab colour
chosen in a dialogue box.<P>
<DT>Printing refill pages
<DD>Print a week on a page the size of an organiser refill (a Filofax "personal" page is 95 by 171
millimetres), with the days laid out as the paper is ruled.<P>
<DT>Keeping in step with another calendar
<DD>Export to iCalendar, import from it: that's all a simple <I>sync</I> is, if each entry keeps the
<CODE>UID</CODE> it had in the other program, so it can be matched up next time.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>The end of Part Two</H2>
Over these seven chapters the Organiser has used everything from Part One - the model and its events, the
list, forms, the toolbar, documents, formats and choices - and added what a bigger application needs:
a common interface for its sections, so one window can hold six different kinds of page; dates; drawing a
whole interface on a canvas; and working with the rest of the desktop and other programs.<P>
Chapter 17 is a last look under the bonnet: how a text area like the Notepad's works, by building one.<P>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Alarms are checked with <CODE>task.every</CODE>: work out each alarm's time, show it once, snooze by
giving it a new time. Old alarms are missed, not shown.<P>
<LI><CODE>Date</CODE>'s <CODE>setHours</CODE> carries minutes and hours over, so time arithmetic can be left
to it.<P>
<LI>Find is shared out: each section has a <CODE>find</CODE> method returning results with a
<CODE>ref</CODE>, and a <CODE>ListView</CODE> shows them.<P>
<LI>Drawing code that only draws can draw on a canvas of its own - <CODE>document.createElement('canvas')</CODE>
- to print through !Printers with <CODE>os.printers.print({ title, canvas })</CODE>.<P>
<LI>iCalendar is lines of <CODE>NAME:value</CODE>, like vCard; <CODE>toolkit/ICal</CODE> reads and writes
appointments, to-dos, repeats and alarms (appendix E).<P>
<LI>Dropped files are recognised by what's in them.<P>
<LI>Destructuring can assign to existing variables and properties, and <CODE>...rest</CODE> collects what's
left of an object.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Make Snooze's five minutes a choice (5, 10 or 15) in the Choices box.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Make Find ignore entries in the diary more than a year ago.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add <I>Menu &gt; File &gt; Export &gt; Addresses (CSV)</I>, using <CODE>csvWrite</CODE> from
chapter 8.<P>
<DT><DD>4  The alarm box doesn't say which organiser the appointment is in. Add the file's leaf name to its
message when more than one organiser is open.<P>
<DT><DD>5  Add the first-day-of-the-week choice described in <I>Going further</I>.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a field to the Choices form, <CODE>{ name: 'snooze', label: 'Snooze', choices: ['5', '10',
'15'] }</CODE>, a default of <CODE>snooze: 5</CODE> in <CODE>DEFAULTS</CODE>, and store it as a number. Then
in <CODE>Reminders</CODE> use it in place of <CODE>SNOOZE</CODE>:
<PRE>
const mins = this.app.prefs.snooze ?? SNOOZE;
this.snoozed.set(name, new Date(Date.now() + mins * 60000));
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  In <CODE>DiaryPage</CODE>'s <CODE>find</CODE>, filter on the date too. Day keys compare as
strings, so:
<PRE>
const since = D.addYears(D.today(), -1);
return this.doc.diary.all()
  .filter((a) =&gt; a.date &gt;= since &amp;&amp;
    a.text.toLowerCase().includes(t))
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  In <CODE>exportMenu</CODE>, import <CODE>csvWrite</CODE> too and add
<PRE>
{ text: 'Addresses (CSV)', submenu: box('Cards/csv', 0xDFE,
  () =&gt; csvWrite(doc.book.all())) },
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  <CODE>check</CODE> knows the document (<CODE>doc</CODE>); pass it to <CODE>show</CODE> and add
<CODE>vfs.leaf(doc.filename)</CODE> to the message when <CODE>this.app.docs.size &gt; 1</CODE> (import
<CODE>vfs</CODE> from <CODE>'riscos'</CODE>).<P>
<DT><DD>5  Give <CODE>startOfWeek</CODE> a second parameter, the first day, counted as <CODE>weekday</CODE>
counts (0 for Monday up to 6 for Sunday), defaulting to Monday:
<PRE>
export const startOfWeek = (key, first = 0) =&gt;
  addDays(key, -((weekday(key) - first + 7) % 7));
</PRE>
Add <CODE>week: 'Monday'</CODE> to the choices, and pass <CODE>prefs.week === 'Sunday' ? 6 : 0</CODE>
wherever the Diary calls <CODE>startOfWeek</CODE>, and change the days' names along the top of the week and
month views to match. Don't
forget the month grid, which starts its rows on the same day.<P>
</DL>

